# allowed_signers — the keys permitted to sign this project's commits and tags.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. `git verify-commit` and `git verify-tag` need to be told which keys
# to accept. GitHub's "Verified" badge uses keys registered on the maintainer's GitHub
# account, which is a DIFFERENT source; this file is what lets you verify without
# trusting that badge. Two independent paths, deliberately.
#
# HOW TO USE IT, from a clone of this repository:
#
#     git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile .github/allowed_signers
#     git verify-tag v0.1.9
#
# The path is relative, so run it from the repository root.
#
# APPEND, NEVER REMOVE. A key leaving this file makes every past signature it made
# unverifiable. Retired keys stay listed precisely so history keeps verifying. Losing a
# private key costs you the ability to make NEW signatures with it and nothing else.
#
# ONE KEY PER MACHINE is the convention here, rather than one key copied around. Each
# machine's key never leaves it, a compromised machine revokes one line instead of the
# whole identity, and this file is multi-key by design. Adding a machine is one line.
#
# IF A KEY IS COMPROMISED, as opposed to lost, removing it from this file is NOT the
# fix: it would silently invalidate honest history alongside the forged commits. Use an
# `ssh-keygen -k` revocation list with `gpg.ssh.revocationFile`, which marks the key as
# revoked while leaving the record of what it signed intact. See SECURITY.md.
#
# `namespaces="git"` restricts each key to git signing, so a key accepted here cannot be
# used to verify signatures made for some other purpose.

